                <h2>Background Problem</h2>
<p>Many marketing teams encounter this confusion: Creative A has 80% video completion rate and 2.3% CTR, with impressive metrics; Creative B only has 35% completion rate and 0.8% CTR, looking ordinary. But after two weeks, Creative A generated zero conversions while Creative B contributed 60% of orders.</p>
<p>This is the difference between "runnable" and "convertible."</p>
<p>This article outlines a review SOP to help you identify issues and adjust budget allocation promptly during the creative lifecycle.</p>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 1: Data Collection — List These 6 Core Metrics</h2>
<p>Before reviewing, confirm your data scope. Export from the ads manager using a unified time period (3-7 days post-launch recommended to avoid cold start fluctuations).</p>
<p>Metrics to collect:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Video completion rate</td>
<td>Judge if creative is "captivating"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CTR (Click-through rate)</td>
<td>Judge if creative is "compelling"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPM</td>
<td>Judge platform's initial rating of creative</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPC (Cost per click)</td>
<td>Judge traffic quality</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conversion events (Add to cart/Checkout)</td>
<td>Judge if creative "converts"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ROAS / CPA</td>
<td>Judge final ROI</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: Compare at least 3 similar creatives horizontally. Single creative data has no reference value.</p>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 2: Distinguishing Features — Runnable ≠ Convertible</h2>
<h3>Typical Features of "Runnable" Creatives</h3>
<ul>
<li>Strong visual impact or suspense in first 3 seconds</li>
<li>Large subtitles, fast-paced music</li>
<li>Prominent CTA text (Click, Download, Shop Now, etc.)</li>
<li>High completion rate, low CPM</li>
<li>But sparse conversion events, erratic CPA</li>
</ul>
<p>These creatives excel at "click-baiting" — users are hooked in but find the product mismatch, then bounce immediately.</p>
<h3>Typical Features of "Convertible" Creatives</h3>
<ul>
<li>Opening may not be eye-catching, but product benefits are clear</li>
<li>Real usage scenarios or user testimonials</li>
<li>CTA matches landing page content</li>
<li>CTR may be lower, but conversion rate (CVR) is stable</li>
<li>7-day ROAS outperforms 3-day data (indicating normal decision cycle)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key Logic</strong>: </p>
<blockquote>
<p>High completion + high CTR, but high CPA → Runnable but not convertible<br />
Medium completion, medium CTR, but stable CVR → Has convertible potential</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 3: Decision Execution — Four-Quadrant Budget Allocation</h2>
<p>After gathering data, make budget decisions using this quadrant framework:</p>
<h3>Quadrant 1: High Runnable + High Convertible (Double High)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Features: Completion &gt;50%, CTR &gt;1.5%, CVR &gt;3%, ROAS &gt;2</li>
<li>Decision: Scale up, test more similar variations</li>
</ul>
<h3>Quadrant 2: High Runnable + Low Convertible (Run Win, Sales Lose)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Features: High completion, high CTR, but few conversions</li>
<li>Decision: Check landing page alignment; if landing page is fine, reduce budget or pause</li>
<li>Note: These creatives easily mislead newcomers into adding budget — stay alert</li>
</ul>
<h3>Quadrant 3: Low Runnable + High Convertible (Run Lose, Sales Win)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Features: Flat data but stable orders, 7-day ROAS continuously rising</li>
<li>Decision: Optimize opening hook, test different thumbnails, don't pause directly</li>
</ul>
<h3>Quadrant 4: Low Runnable + Low Convertible (Double Low)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Features: All metrics below average</li>
<li>Decision: Pause directly, conserve budget</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Additional Note: Timing Window Matters</h2>
<p>Creative performance changes over time:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Days 1-3</strong>: Platform exploring audience, data volatile, avoid drawing conclusions</li>
<li><strong>Days 4-7</strong>: Model initially converged, review can begin</li>
<li><strong>Days 8-14</strong>: Judge if main creatives warrant continued investment</li>
</ul>
<p>If a creative starts poorly but suddenly picks up on Day 4, it may be effective in specific audiences — observe 2-3 more days before deciding.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Checklist</h2>
<p>At the end of each review, go through this checklist:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Is the data period unified (3-7 days)?</li>
<li>[ ] Are at least 3 creatives compared?</li>
<li>[ ] Are both runnable metrics (completion, CTR) and convertible metrics (CVR, ROAS) collected?</li>
<li>[ ] Has landing page factor interference been ruled out?</li>
<li>[ ] Has the current creative's quadrant been determined?</li>
<li>[ ] Is the budget adjustment decision documented?</li>
</ul>
<p>Completing these steps gives your creative review clear action guidance — no more budget adjustments based on gut feeling.</p>
